## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of emerging porous materials, consisting of periodically connected organic linkers and inorganic connectors of metal ions/clusters.[1] The framework structures of MOFs are majorly defined by the orientation of coordination donors in rigid organic linkers and the coordination geometry of metal connectors. The connections between these building synthons lead to the formation of different framework structures with large and accessible inside pore space. The most attractive aspect of MOF materials lies in the facts that they not only can retain the individual functions, but also often exhibit novel properties derived from blending of the individuals. Accompanying the development of the state-of-the-art characterization technologies and the multiple interdisciplinary collaborative research, the framework structures and functions of MOF materials have been reached to the domain of systematic control and rational modification by in situ self-assembly or post-modification. Due to the reversibly self-correcting kinetic nature controlled by the moderate coordination bond energies, MOF materials exhibit unique features, which cannot be matched by the traditional materials: i) clearly defined crystal structures consisting of periodically connected constituent moieties; ii) adjustable framework structures including pore structures and sizes, and surface areas; iii) modifiable hydrophobic and hydrophilic pore nature to facilitate selective recognition, binding and adsorption of different guest molecules; iv) tunable functions to realize applications in different fields; v) special confined nanopore microenvironments to enhance the inherent properties of individual constituents. Being greatly developed over the past two decades, numerous applications of MOF materials have been realized in the fields of gas storage and separation, molecular recognition, magnetics, biomedicine, sensing, nonlinear optics, ferroelectrics, and catalysis.[2–10]
In terms of catalytic application, MOF materials have the following superior characters: i) the prescribed catalytic moieties can be either immobilized on the pore surfaces or encapsulated in the inside pore space; ii) the uniform dispersion and the high density of catalytic sites can improve the utility; iii) the periodically dispersed active sites in the pore surface/space can synergistically work to improve the catalytic efficiency; iv) the appropriate hydrophilic and hydrophobic nature of inside pores can facilitate the interactions between MOF matrices and reactant molecules, which have the capability of enriching reactant molecules to improve the catalytic efficiency; v) the confined pore sizes can selectively transport different substrate molecules for size-selective catalysis; vi) the nanosized pore space provides special catalytic microenvironments, such as quantum size confinement effect, stereochemical confinement and electrondeficient or electron-rich circumstance, to tune the reactivity of reactant molecules; vii) the active moieties in the porous microenvironment can facilitate the effective contact between catalytic sites and reactant molecules to improve the catalytic efficiency; viii) the assistant functional moieties on the pore surfaces can collaboratively work with active sites to improve the catalytic efficiency; ix) the metastable catalytic moieties can be stabilized by immobilizing or trapping in the porous frameworks to maintain long term catalytic efficiency, including to avoid suicidal self-reaction, self-accumulation and decomposition; x) the solid catalysts are easily recovered and reused for the successive runs with retained catalytic efficiency. In other word, MOF materials with high porosity, high surface area, and tunable hydrophobic and hydrophilic pore nature exhibit certain enzyme-like features for highly efficient catalysis under mild conditions.[10] Moreover, the clearly defined crystal structures of MOFs and catalytic site locations on/in the pore surface/space provide direct visualization of the binding interactions between catalytic sites and reactant molecules for comprehensive understanding of the catalytic mechanisms. The above features of MOF materials endow them as a class of very promising heterogeneous catalysts for different reactions, which represents an attractive and upsurging field promoted by the multiple interdisciplinary researches among organic, inorganic, catalytic and materials chemistry, and so on.[9,10]
Under feasible strategies, numerous molecular catalysts have been successfully incorporated into porous MOFs. Even though the MOF catalysts are still under a developing stage, however, the recent works demonstrate that the emerging porous catalysts exhibit super catalytic properties, such as high activity, stability, selectivity, simple recovery and easy recycling, which cannot be matched by the traditional molecular catalysts and solid catalysts. The investigation on MOF catalysts reviewed herein is mainly focused on: i) the synthetic strategies on MOF catalysts, ii) the catalytic site dependent catalytic properties, and iii) the roles of collaborative sites, iv) the roles of pore microenvironments, and v) the roles of transmission channels on the distinct catalytic properties of MOF catalysts. We will describe the strengths and weaknesses of the catalytic sites derived from different essential constituents of MOFs, such as metal nodes, organic bridging ligands with incorporated catalytic sites, and inside pore space with encapsulated molecular catalysts. We hope that this review will offer comprehensive understanding of the MOF catalysts and inspire much more interdisciplinary researches on this upsurging field.
