Thanks to the advances in synthetic strategies and characterization technologies on MOF materials, the catalytic properties of the crystalline solids can be rationally designed and controlled in depth. Besides of the generally required permanent porosity and substrate accessible nanopore space, the catalytic properties of MOFs are majorly dependent on the properties of constituent moieties, the accessibility of catalytic sites, and the hydrophobic and hydrophilic nature of inside pores. In light of the three essential constituent elements, e.g., metal nodes, organic linkers and activated to generate coordinatively unsaturated metal sites by removing the labile solvent molecules under suitable conditions. iii) There are abundant coordinative defect sites of metal nodes on the exterior solid surfaces of MOFs for catalysis. However, these coordinative defect sites of metal nodes are easily blocked during catalysis, which will significantly deteriorate the catalytic efficiency.[11]
Because metal nodes are the pivot constituent elements of MOFs, the chemical combination between metal nodes and organic substrates often leads to the framework structures collapse during catalysis. For example, the well-known porous MOF HKUST-1 is built from paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ secondary building units (SBUs) linked by benzene 1,3,5-tricarb­ oxylate (BTC) (Figure 2).[12] The axial coordination sites of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are occupied by weakly bound water molecules, which can be easily removed by simply heating the compound in vacuum. Dehydration activation makes the copper Lewis acid centers available to combine with organic substrates for catalysis, such as cyanosilylation of aldehydes, isomerization of terpene derivatives, and Pechmann condensation of naphthol and ethyl acetoacetate.[13] However, because the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are easily reduced by reductive reactant molecules, such as aldehydes, under the catalytic conditions, the framework structure of HKUST-1 frequently collapsed during catalysis.
On the other hand, even though the framework structures of MOFs are very stable, however, because the metal nodes often locate in the pore corners, they are difficultly accessed to reactant molecules. For example, the highly stable MOF UiO-66 is built from $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}$ clustered SBUs linked by terephthalate (bdc) ligands, consisting of nanopore space for encapsulation of reactant molecules (Figure 3).[14] UiO-66 can be easily activated by dehydroxylation without collapse of the framework structure. The activated UiO-66, containing catalytically active $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ clusters, has been employed as a catalyst on the cross aldol condensation reaction between heptanal and benzaldehyde. Because the Lewis acid catalytic sites located in the pore cage corners are not easily reached to the included substrate molecules, the substrate conversion is quite low $(30\%)$ .[15] Moreover, using metal nodes of MOFs as catalytic sites is full of opportunism, since the coordination sites of metal nodes are not always available for bonding with reactant molecules, whereas the solid surface promoted catalytic efficiency, based on the coordinative defect sites of metal ions, is very low.
One of the most rational and straightforward strategies is to incorporate catalytic sites into organic linkers. Orthogonal separation of the catalytic sites and the coordination donor sites in organic linkers means that the participation of active sites in catalysis does not affect the connection bonds between metal nodes and coordination donors of organic linkers, and thus to maintain the framework structures of MOFs during catalysis. Moreover, the catalytic sites are easily accessed to reactant molecules by immobilizing on the pore surfaces of MOFs. To date, numerous catalytic moieties have been successfully immobilized onto the pore surfaces of MOFs by this strategy, which present remarkable catalytic efficiency with interesting sizeand shape-selective properties.
Because there remain some difficulties for in situ control of the functions of porous MOF materials according to the state-of-the-art technologies, it is impossible to introduce any molecular catalyst into the crystalline porous materials by “onepot” synthesis method. Therefore, post-modification is emerged as one of the alternative strategies to realize MOF catalysis.[16] The post-modification techniques of MOFs include complexing of active metal sites via the remaining available coordination donors in organic linkers, grafting of catalytic sites onto the coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes, modifying of organic ligands with catalytic moieties, and encapsulating of molecular catalysts in the interior pore space. Theoretically speaking, almost all molecular catalysts can be encapsulated into the internal void space of MOFs under suitable strategies. Recent results demonstrate that the post-modification strategy represents one of the most promising methods to realize MOF catalysis because there is no special requirement on the host-guest interaction and reactivity between MOF matrices and molecular catalyst guests.
