According to the above discussions, using metal nodes as catalytic sites is full of opportunism since the metal node-based catalytic properties of MOFs cannot be rationally controlled in situ synthesis. Nevertheless, there have been numerous MOFs containing potential active metal sites for catalysis. Overviews of the structures of known MOFs, it has been found that the MOFs, consisting of clustered metal nodes, often contain coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, in which part metal coordination sites combine with organic linkers and the remaining metal coordination sites are accessible to reactant molecules for catalysis. For example, the highly porous MOF MIL-101 $\textstyle(\mathrm{Cr})$ , [Cr3X(H2O)2O(bdc)3] (X = F, OH), is built from trimeric CrIII clusters connected to bdc ligands with the dimensions of pore cages and pore windows up to 34 and $16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ respectively (Figure 4).[17] In the structure of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , the axial coordination sites of $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions are occupied by weakly bound water molecules, which can be easily removed by simply heating the compound in vacuum. The active $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ sites located on the pore surfaces of dehydrated MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ are accessible to the incoming reactant molecules for catalysis, such as cyanosilylation of aldehydes, oxidation of hydrocarbons and sulfides, and cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to epoxides.[18]
