## 3. Catalytic Sites Incorporated in Different Constituent Elements of MOFs
## 3.1. Metal Nodes as Catalytic Sites
Driven by the strong coordination bond energy associating with metal nodes and organic connectors, the coordination sites of metal nodes are often fully ligated by organic linkers in MOFs, which let the metal coordination sites inaccessible to organic substrates for catalysis. However, constrained by the directionality of rigid organic ligands and the coordination geometry of metal nodes, partial coordination sites of metal nodes are often inaccessible to the coordination donors of organic linkers. The remaining coordination sites of metal nodes are either coordinatively vacant or coordinating to volatile $\mathrm{[Mn_{4}C l]^{7+}}$ units linked by $\mathsf{b t t}^{3-}$ ligands with the pore dimensions ranging from 7 to $10\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 5). The unsaturated coordination sites of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions in Mn-btt are readily accessible to the included reactant molecules for catalytic applications, such as cyanosilylation of aromatic aldehydes and the Mukaiyama-aldol reaction. IR spectra confirmed that the activation of reactant molecules occurs on the coordinatively unsaturated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ sites. Because the catalysis mainly occurs inside the pore space, Mn-btt exhibits size-selective catalytic property in the cyanosilylation reaction with different ketone substrates and the Mukaiyama-aldol reaction with different silyl enolates.
It has been known that different organic ligands present different coordination modes on combination with metal ions, which often result in different metal coordination spheres in situ synthesis of MOFs. Therefore, using mixed organic ligands for the synthesis of porous MOFs often results in different metal coordination spheres in one porous MOF, in which partial metal sites are either coordinatively vacant or coordinating to volatile solvent molecules. In the structure of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(p d t c)}$ $\mathrm{(pvbc)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ ·guest $\mathrm{\Delta[H_{4}p d t c=}$ pyridine-2,3,5,6-tetracarboxylic acid; Hpvbc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ (E)-4-(2-(pyridin-4-yl)vinyl)benzoic acid), two kinds of pyridine carboxylate ligands are connecting with copper $(\mathrm{II})$ nodes to form a porous framework with 1D channels of $\mathbf{\bar{1}3.7\:\bar{A}}\times23.6\:\mathrm{\AA}$ in dimensions.[20] As shown in Figure 6, the coordination sites of one third copper ions are partially occupied by volatile water molecules exposed on the interior pore surfaces, which are readily activated under suitable conditions as demonstrated by an SCSC structural transformation experiment. $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{3}(p d t c)(p v b c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}]}}$ is highly active in the catalytic Henry reaction between benzaldehydes and nitroalkanes with product yield up to $85\%$ . The catalytic property is much superior to that of the MOF precursors, such as simple copper(II) salts, because the active metal coordination sites are easily blocked by counter ions in homogeneous phase. $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{pdtc})$ $\mathrm{(pvbc)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ is very stable, and can be simply recovered and reused for five successive runs with retained structural integrity and catalytic activity.
## 3.2. Catalytic Sites Incorporated in Organic Ligands
The most attractive feature of MOF catalysts lies in the potential to incorporate the prescribed catalytic sites in organic ligands. Based on this strategy, large void space can be easily produced in the vicinity of catalytic sites. The catalytic sites, immobilized on the pore surfaces of MOFs, are freely contacted to the incoming reactant molecules, and thus to realize highly efficient catalysis.
## 3.2.1. Metalloporphyrins as Catalytic Sites
Metalloporphyrins are the active sites of monooxygenases which can efficiently oxidize a variety of organic molecules under mild conditions.[21] The rigid macrocycle structures of metalloporphyrins make them as a class of ideal building blocks for the construction of porous MOFs. Immobilization of the biomimetic catalytic sites into porous MOFs is able to stabilize the catalytic moieties, create the collaborative porous microenvironments, improve the interactions between catalytic sites and reactant molecules, and facilitate the substrate recognition, which can synergistically enhance the catalytic efficiency with interesting shape- and size-selective catalytic properties. Moreover, the catalytic properties of porphyrinic MOFs can be easily tuned by modulating the porphyrin metal catalytic sites and tailoring the peripheral pore microenvironments, which have been well demonstrated by the recent biomimetic catalysis.[10]
Hupp and co-workers have controlled the introduction of different porphyrins and different porphyrin metal ions (e.g., $\mathsf{A l}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Pd^{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , and $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{III}}}}$ ) in a series of isostructural porous porphyrinic MOFs (Figure 7).[22] In these porous porphyrinic MOFs, the porphyrin metal sites are readily accessible to the reactant molecules transported through the open channels. The porphyrinic MOF, consisting of $\mathrm{{Mn^{III}}}$ -porphyrins as catalytic sites, is highly efficient for the styrene epoxidation by the 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl)iodosylbenzene oxidant. The porphyrinic MOF is very stable, and can last for 2150 turnovers without deactivation, whereas the homogeneous 5,10,15,20- tetra­kis(pentafluorophenyl)porphyrin- $.\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{Cl})$ catalyst is deactivated after 780 turnovers due to catalyst self-oxidation. The porphyrinic MOF can also oxidize cyclohexane into cyclohexanol and cyclohexanone (83:17 alcohol/ketone ratio) in $20\%$ total yield (based on the oxidant).
