## 3.2.2. Metallosalens as Catalytic Sites
Salens, the condensation products of aryl aldehydes and diamines, are a class of universal and excellent chelate ligands, which can combine with various metal ions with different catalytic properties for highly efficient catalysis.[33] Moreover, chiral metallosalens are a class of excellent asymmetric catalysts which have been used to prompt a variety of reactions with high enantioselectivities. Chiral metallosalens represent a class of attractive building synthons for the synthesis of homochiral porous MOF materials, and thus to realize highly efficient asymmetric catalysis with high enantioselectivity.
Salen MOFs are dependent on the synthetic conditions and the dimensions of organic ligands. Controlled by the sizes of Salen bridging ligands and the interpenetration folds, their largest open channel dimensions are ranging from $8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}\times6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ to 25 Å $\Dot{\times2}3\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . These CMOFs display distinct catalytic activities on the asymmetric epoxidation of olefin 2,2-dimethyl-2H-chromene with 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl)iodosylbenzene oxidant, in which the conversion rate increases accompanying the pore size increase. The CMOF catalysts are recyclable with slight deterioration in conversions and enantioselectivities, and retain their framework structures after catalytic epoxidation of olefins.
