The Cui group developed a Co-Salen-based metal–organic framework, $[\mathrm{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Salen}))_{4}(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}]\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ building from $\mathrm{Cd}_{4}$ clusters and Co-Salen carboxylate linkers with open chiral channels of about $12\mathrm{~\AA~}\times8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in dimensions.[35] The Co-Salen catalytic sites, immobilized on the pore surfaces, are accessible to the reactant molecules transported through the open channels. The Co-Salen MOF exhibits high catalytic efficiency on the asymmetric hydrolytic kinetic resolution of a range of benzyloxy epoxide derivatives with ee (enantiomeric excess) value up to $99.5\%$ . Since the heterogeneous catalysis mainly occurs inside the pore space of the Co-Salen MOF, it exhibits sizeselective catalytic property because the bulky substrates have difficulty accessing the catalytic sites on the inside pore surfaces. For examples, the conversion of small substrate phenoxy epoxide is of $56\%$ , whereas the conversion of sterically bulky substrate triphenyl glycidyl ether is less than $5\%$ under the same catalytic conditions. Recycling experiments demonstrate that the CoSalen MOF catalyst is very stable, and can be simply recovered and reused five times without significant loss of catalytic activity.
## 3.2.3. Organic Moieties as Catalytic Sites
There are a few proofs of concept with regard to the organic catalytic sites immobilized in porous MOF materials. The organic catalytic sites in MOFs are either incorporated in organic ligands or terminated on metal nodes. The Kitagawa group reported a porous MOF $\mathrm{[Cd}(4{\cdot}\mathrm{btapa})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ ·guest (Cd-btapa), building from a tridentate amide ligand 4-btapa $(N^{1},N^{3},N^{5}-$ tri(pyridin-4-yl)benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxamide) connected with $\mathrm{Cd^{II}}$ centers (Figure 15).[36] In the porous framework of Cdbtapa, the $\mathrm{Cd^{II}}$ center acts as a six-connected node and 4-btapa as a three-connected linker to result in 1D channels of $4.7\mathrm{~\bar{A}~}$ $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\times~}7.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in dimensions. The amide groups, highly ordered on the pore surfaces, functionalize as catalytic sites by utilizing two types of hydrogen bonding sites of the electron acceptor $(\mathrm{-NH})$ moieties and the electron donor $(-\mathrm{C=O})$ groups. Cdbtapa catalyzes the Knoevenagel condensation reaction between benzaldehyde and malononitrile with conversion of $98\%$ , which is much superior to the desolvated sample of Cd-btapa and the MOF precursors of 4-btapa ligand and $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Because the catalysis mainly occurs inside the pore space, the bulky substrates are almost unreactive, which demonstrates that Cd-btapa is a substrate-selective catalyst. Cd-btapa is easily recovered and reused for successive runs with retained catalytic activity.
Hupp and co-workers reported a urea-containing MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}(\mathrm{cada})$ (NU-601; bpy $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine; $\mathrm{\:H_{4}c a d a\:\:=\:}$ $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(carbonylbis(azanediyl))diisophthalic acid), building from mixed ligands of carboxylate and pyridine derivatives, in which the urea N–H bonds on cada ligands are projecting into the substrate accessible pore space (Figure 16).[37] NU-601 acts as a hydrogen bond donor catalyst for the Friedel–Crafts reaction between pyrroles and nitroalkenes with substrate conversion up to $98\%$ . Compared with the molecular catalyst diphenylurea, NU-601 displays an increased rate enhancement, because the catalytic efficiency of molecular diphenylurea is highly limited by the self-quenching behavior. NU-601 is reusable, experiencing a slight degradation of reactivity after five catalytic cycles with retained crystallinity.
Pyrrolidines, a class of proline-derivatives, have been widely employed as efficient organocatalysts on the asymmetric aldol reactions. The Telfer group used (S)-2-(1-(tert-butoxycarbonyl) pyrrolidine-2-carboxamido)-[1,1′-biphenyl]-4,4′-dicarboxylic acid ((S)-pro-boc) as a bridging ligand, in which the pyrrolidine moiety is incorporated in the 2 or $2^{\prime}$ position of biphenyl-4,4′-dicarboxylic acid, to connect with $\mathrm{Zn^{II}}$ ions for the formation of cubic crystals of IRMOF-pro-boc.[38] IRMOF-pro-boc is easily deprotected to form catalytically active IRMOF-pro in an SCSC manner by microwave irradiation heating (Figure 17). IRMOF-pro is an efficient catalyst on the asymmetric aldol reaction between acetone and 4-nitrobenzaldehyde, which results the aldol product of 4-hydroxy-4-(4-nitrophenyl)-pentan-2-one with an ee value of $29\%$
## 3.3. Catalytic Moieties Encapsulated in the Pore Space
The inherent porous nature of MOF materials allows us immobilization of various catalytic moieties in the pore space. Confining molecular catalysts in the pore space of MOF materials is a very promising strategy, which can integrate the super functions of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts within one porous material.
## 3.3.1. Metalloporphyrins Trapped in the Pore Space
Encapsulating porphyrins and metalloporphyrins in the confined pore space of MOFs is an alternative strategy to realize highly efficient biomimetic catalysis by combining the unique natures of metalloporphyrins and MOFs. Alkordi et al. have encapsulated porphyrin 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(1-methyl-4-pyridinio)porphyrin $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TMPyP})$ molecules in the pores of an indium imidazoledicarboxylate-based rho-ZMOF in a “shipin-a-bottle” configuration (denoted as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TMPyP@}r h o{\mathrm{-}}Z\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{-}}$ ) (Figure 18).[39] The catalytic properties of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TMPyP}@r h o.$ ZMOF are readily tunable by post-metalation of the metal-free porphyrin moieties with different metal ions, such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\dot{Z}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ ions. For example, the manganese(III) ion post-metalated material Mn-TMPyP@rho-ZMOF is highly efficient on the oxidation of cyclohexane to cyclohexanol/cyclohexanone with $91.5\%$ yield (based on the oxidant) and 23.5 TON. Larsen et al. developed a Fe4SP@HKUST-1 platform, consisting of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -tetrakis(4-sulphonatophenyl)porphyrin (Fe4SP) guests encapsulated in the pore space of HKUST-1, by the “ship-in-a-bottle” strategy.[40] Fe4SP $@$ HKUST-1 was applied to mimic the catalytic behaviors of peroxidase enzyme by using $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azinodi(3-ethylbenzthiazoline)-6-sulfonate (ABTS) as a redox indicator to the addition of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ . The catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}4\mathrm{SP}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ is comparable to that of micro­ peroxidase-11 (MP-11) enzyme and homogeneous molecular Fe4SP.
## 3.3.2. Polyoxometalates Encapsulated in the Pore Space
Song et al. developed a strategy to encapsulate the Keggin-type POM $\mathrm{[CuPW_{11}O_{39}]^{5-}}$ in the pore cages of HKUST-1, which resulted in a composite hybrid material $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{6})_{2}]_{4}[\{(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{4}\mathrm{N}\}_{4}\mathrm{CuPW}_{11}\mathrm{O}_{39}\mathrm{H}]{\cdot}40\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(CuPW_{11}O_{39}@}$ HKUST-1) by “one-pot” synthesis method (Figure 19).[41] It is interesting that the stability of both HKUST-1 host and POM guests in the composite material is significantly improved, because close matching of the POM diameter and the MOF pore size results in a synergistic stabilization effect. The composite material $\mathrm{CuPW_{11}O_{39}@H K U S T–1}$ demonstrates high catalytic efficiency on the reaction $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}+1/2\mathrm{O}_{2}\to1/8\mathrm{S}_{8}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with TON up to 4300. The catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{CuPW}_{11}\mathrm{O}_{39}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ is much superior to that of the single individuals $\mathrm{[CuPW_{11}O_{39}]^{5-}}$ and HKUST-1. Recycling experiments, FTIR spectroscopy, and PXRD demonstrate that $\mathrm{CuPW_{11}O_{39}@H K U S T.1}$ stays intact, including catalytic activity, structural components, and structural integrity. The work demonstrates that synergizing the functions from different structural components can result in unusual catalytic efficiency.
Liu et al. reported a facile and efficient method to obtain the cubic and octahedral crystal forms of NENU-3a consisting of a $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}_{2}$ scaffold and encapsulated phosphotungstic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40})$ (HPW) (Figure 20).[42] The morphology of NENU-3a was produced by coordination modulation with a modulator, p-toluic acid. Under optimized conditions, the octahedral o-NENU-3a characterized by only {111} facets could be transformed into its cube-shaped counterpart, c-NENU3a, bounded entirely by $\{100\}$ facets. The catalytic activity of NENU-3a was much enhanced for the catalytic esterification reaction, when the crystal morphology was transformed from an octahedral shape into cubic form. For example, the cubic crystals of c-NENU-3a can efficiently prompt biodiesel production, and the conversion of fatty acids (C12–C22) is much higher than for those using octahedral morphological o-NENU-3a with $590\%$ and $<22\%$ yields, respectively. The c-NENU-3a catalyst is very stable, and can be easily recovered and reused for five reaction cycles with retained structure, composition and catalytic activity. This work demonstrates that the MOF morphology plays critical roles regarding the catalytic efficiency, and provides a convenient pathway to enhance facet exposure of catalytic sites, and thus to improve the catalytic efficiency, especially for the bulky reactant molecules that find it difficult to access the catalytic sites inside the pore space of MOF catalysts.
The Lin group developed a charge-assisted self-assembly strategy for the encapsulation of POM clusters in the pore space of MOFs.[43] The cationic phosphorescent MOF $[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{bdc}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Ru})_{6}](\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{12}$ is built from $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ SBUs connected with dicarboxylate (bdc-Ru) ligands containing $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ chromophores (Figure 21). The Wells–Dawson-type
POM $[\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}$ can be in situ encapsulated in the pore space to form a series of $\operatorname{POM}@\operatorname{UiO}$ materials with different POM contents. In these composite materials, $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ units perform as light-harvesting moieties and POM clusters as photocatalytic sites. POM $@$ UiOs are highly active for the visible-light-driven proton reduction reaction in the presence of methanol or triethanolamine as the sacrificial electron donor. No $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production was detected from the homogeneous pairs of $[\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}/[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ or $[\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}/\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}{\cdot}\mathrm{R}\mathrm{u}$ with methanol as the sacrificial electron donor. The POM $@$ UiO can be simply recovered and reused for three cycles with slight loss of activity and crystallinity. This work demonstrates that encapsulation of the redox-active POM clusters in the pore space of photoactive MOFs can facilitate the multielectron injection from photosensitizing chromophores to POM moieties by hierarchical organization of the multiple functional components in one porous material.
## 4. Post-Modification of MOFs with Catalytic Moieties
## 4.1. Grafting of Catalytic Moieties on Metal Nodes
By post-synthetic modification of MIL-101(Cr) with chiral ligands (S)-N-(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide and (S)-N-(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide, the Kim group successfully immobilized the pyrrolidine catalytic moieties onto the coordination sites of $\mathrm{Cr^{III}}$ metal nodes.[44] The post-modified materials demonstrate high catalytic efficiency on the asymmetric aldol reaction between aromatic aldehydes and ketones, and high stability for recycling without much change in yield and enantioselectivity. The yields of aldol products are ranging from 60 to $90\%$ with enantioselectivity up to $80\%$ .
## 4.2. Post-Modification of Organic Ligands with Catalytic Moieties
IRMOF-3 $\mathrm{(Zn_{4}O(b d c\cdot N H_{2})_{3})}$ is isostructural to MOF-5 with pore diameter of $9.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 22), in which the benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate ligand is decorated with an amine group $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ .[45] Corma and co-workers have post-modified IRMOF-3 by reacting the amine groups with salicylaldehyde molecules to produce Schiff base moieties, and further reacting with $\mathsf{A u}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions to generate $\mathsf{A u}^{\mathrm{III}}$ Schiff base catalytic sites on the pore surfaces of IRMOF-3.[46] The post-synthesized material efficiently prompts the coupling reaction among N-tosyl protected ethynylaniline, paraformaldehyde, and piperidine with substrate conversion up to $93\%$ .
$N$ -Heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) are a class of electron-rich, neutral $\sigma\mathrm{.}$ donor ligands, which can form strong coordination bonds with various metal ions for different catalytic reactions.[47] The $\mathtt{w u}$ group has successfully incorporated NHCs into a porous metal–organic nanotube, $[\mathbf{ZnLCl}_{2}]$ ·guest (CarbeneMOF; $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}\ =\ 1,1^{\prime}$ -methylenebis(3-(4-carboxy-2-methylphenyl)- $1H$ -imidazol-3-ium)), which consists of L linking up tetra-coordinated zinc ions (Figure 23).[48] The 3D framework of CarbeneMOF is built from interlocking of the individual nanotubes, which result in 1D helical cylindered channels with diameter of $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The imidazolium moieties are readily available on the pore surfaces for post-metallation to tune the catalytic properties. The palladium-metalated material Carbene-MOFPd acts as a highly active heterogeneous catalyst for a number of reactions, such as Suzuki–Miyaura and Heck coupling reactions (up to $99\%$ yield). Carbene-MOF-Pd is also highly active on the hydrogenation of nitrobenzene and olefins (up to $99\%$ yield). The catalytic efficiency is improved more than 100 times, compared with that of its constituent moieties, such as $\mathrm{Pd}/$ $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathbf{L}$ , ${\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ . Moreover, Carbene-MOF-Pd can be used for hydrogen reduction of large scale olefins, as demonstrated in the reduction of (E)-ethyl cinnamate $(17.6~\mathrm{g})$ with TON up to 13 746. The high catalytic efficiency of the recovered solid catalyst did not deteriorate in the recycling experiments with six successive runs.
The single-crystalline nature of MOFs allows us to examine the relationship between structures and catalytic properties of porous MOFs in details. The Lin group has designed a 1,1′-bi-2-naphthol (BINOL)-based chiral bridging ligand (R)-6,6’-dichloro- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy- $^{\cdot1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (Binol-Py), and further synthesized two homochiral porous MOFs $\mathrm{[Cd_{3}(B i n o l.P y)_{4}(N O_{3})_{6}]}$ ·guest (Cd-Binol-1) and [Cd(Binol$\mathrm{Py})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}][\mathrm{ClO}_{4}]_{2}$ ·guest (Cd-Binol-2) (Figure 24).[49] By taking advantage of the readily accessible chiral dihydroxy groups on the pore surfaces in Cd-Binol-1, the BINOL moieties were reacted with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ to afford (BINOLate) $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{2}$ catalytic sites. The post-modified solid is highly active on the addition reaction of $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ to aromatic aldehydes with complete conversion, which affords chiral secondary alcohols with high ee values (up to $90\%$ ) that rival those of the homogeneous analogs.
However, under the identical conditions, a mixture of CdBinol-2 and ${\mathrm{Ti}}(\mathrm{O}^{i}{\mathrm{Pr}})_{4}$ is inactive on this reaction, even though Cd-Binol-2 is highly porous. Their distinct catalytic activities on the diethyl zinc addition reaction can be simply explained by carefully examining their single-crystal structures. The dihydroxy groups in Cd-Binol-1 are exposed on the pore surfaces, which are freely reacted with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ to form active sites. On the contrary, the steric congestion around the chiral dihydroxy groups in Cd-Binol-2 prevents the access to isopropoxide. This work highlights that the clearly defined crystal structures of MOF catalysts can provide direct visualization on understanding of the catalytic properties of MOFs.
## 4.3. Immobilization of Metal Nanoparticles in the Pore Space
MOF materials provide the confined pore space to prevent the migration and aggregation of metal nanoparticles (MNPs), which can configure the sizes and shapes of MNPs in situ synthesis of MNPs inside the pore space of MOFs (denoted as $\mathsf{M N P s@M O F s})$ .[50] There are several effective strategies which have been developed for the synthesis and immobilization of MNPs inside the pore space of MOFs, including chemical vapor deposition (CVD), solution infiltration, and solid grinding.
By the CVD method, the Fischer group successfully loaded volatile [Ru(cod)(cot)] molecules in the pores of MOF-5 to result in $[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{cod})(\mathrm{cot})]_{3.5}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ .[51] Hydrogenolysis of $[\mathrm{Ru(cod)}$ $(\mathrm{cot})]_{3.5}@\mathrm{MOF}.5$ generated a composite material ${\mathrm{Ru}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{\cdot}5$ in which the ruthenium nanoparticles are embedded in the pore space with particle sizes ranging from 1.5 to $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ . $\mathrm{Ru}@$ MOF-5 was used for the catalytic hydrogenation of benzene to produce cyclohexane ( $25\%$ yield). By simply grinding a mixture of ZIF-8 and volatile organo-gold complex ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{Au}}$ (acac) and subsequent hydrogenation, the $\mathrm{\DeltaXu}$ group supported Au NPs inside the pore space of ZIF-8.[52] $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}\cdot8$ was proved as an efficient catalyst on the CO oxidation reaction. Li and co-workers prepared Pd nanoparticles supported in the pore space of MIL101(Cr) by impregnation method.[53] The $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ catalyst is highly active on the Suzuki–Miyaura and Ullmann coupling reactions of aryl chlorides, and direct synthesis of methyl isobutyl ketone. By a simple liquid impregnation–reduction method, Cheng and co-workers successfully supported Ag NPs in MIL-101(Cr) to yield Ag@MIL-101.[54] $\mathbf{Ag}@\mathbf{ML-}101$ demonstrates excellent catalytic activity for fixation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with terminal alkynes into propiolic acids under mild conditions. The high catalytic efficiency of $\mathbf{Ag}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ is attributed to the outstanding bifunctional characters of simultaneous capture and conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with low energy consumption. $\mathbf{Ag}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ could be easily separated, regenerated and reused for five cycles with almost-retained catalytic activity and stability.
Xu and co-workers developed a “double solvents” strategy to synthesize ultrafine Pt NPs inside the pore space of MIL-101(Cr).[55] The double solvents include hydrophilic water and hydrophobic hexane, in which the hydrophilic water was used to solve the polar metal precursor $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}P t C l_{6}}$ whereas the hydrophobic hexane can suspend the MIL-101(Cr) solid to facilitate the impregnation of polar metal precursor into the pore space. $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ demonstrates high catalytic activities on the reactions of liquid-phase ammonia borane hydrolysis, solid-phase ammonia borane thermal dehydrogenation, and gas-phase CO oxidation (Figure 25).
$\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ favors electron–hole separation and effectively avoids the undesired volume charge recombination, which can improve the charge-carrier utilization and thus photocatalytic hydrogen-production efficiency via water splitting. Moreover, the electron-injected Pt NPs inside the porous MOF are more easily accessible to protons. The $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst possesses excellent stability and recyclability as a result of the great confinement for Pt NPs inside the pore space of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ to avoid aggregation or leaching during the reaction. Pt NPs in $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}}66{\-}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{NH_{2}}}$ are prone to leaching and aggregation, resulting in activity loss. This study provides a comprehensive insight for the development of efficient porous photocatalysts by improving the efficiency of electron–hole separation and charge-carrier utilization.
## 5. Microenvironment-Dependent Catalytic Property
## 5.1. Nanochannel-Confined Polymerization Reaction
Besides of the catalytic properties of active sites, the pore microen  
vironment plays important roles on the catalytic performance of   
porous MOFs, in which the confined pore space can configure the   
shapes and configurations of product molecules.[57] Kitagawa and rkers have carried out the polymerization reaction of methyl   
propiolate (MP) in the nanochannels of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}b p y)]}.$ ,   
in which the carboxylate moieties located in the pore walls act talytic sites, whereas the confined nanochannels direct   
the selective polymerization of substituted acetylene molecules   
(Figure 27).[58] After catalytic polymerization of MP, the frame  
work structure of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}b p y)]}$ remained intact, and the   
polymerized product polyMP was embedded in the MOF chan  
nels, which resulted in a dark-green powder composite mate  
rial $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{-}b p y}$ ]⊃polyMP. The polyMP product can be   
easily released from the host framework by extraction with DMF solvent. The discrete model catalyst, sodium benzoate, is almost inactive on this polymerization reaction under the identical conditions. These results indicate that the MOF catalysis is a characteristic nanochannel-controlled polymerization reaction with interesting features of high acceleration, stereocontrol and monomer selectivity. The solid catalyst is easily isolated from the reaction suspension and reused without loss of activity.
## 5.2. Chiral Microenvironment Dependent Enantioselectivity
By using $N$ -protected l- and d-N-tert-butoxycarboxyl-2-(imidazole)-1-pyrrolidine (l- and d-BCIP) as chiral adducts, the Duan group synthesized two enantiomorphs Ce-MDIP1 and Ce-MDIP2, which are built from coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Ce^{III}}$ ions linked by mdip $\mathrm{[H_{4}m d i p=}$ methylenediisophthalic acid) ligands with 1D chiral channels of $10.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\dot{\times}$ $6.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in dimensions (Figure 28).[59] Due to the weak coordination affinity between N atoms and lanthanide ions, the chiral adducts l- and d-BCIP are not incorporated into the frameworks of Ce-MDIPs, which only act as inducing agents for the formation of chiral coordination networks. Ce-MDIP1 demonstrates excellent catalytic activity on the cyanosilylation of aldehydes with ee value up to $98\%$ . Similarly, Ce-MDIP2 catalyzes the cyanosilylation reaction to result in the enantiomeric products controlled by the Cotton effect. Since the chiral moieties of l- and d-BCIP are not incorporated into CeMDIPs, the enantioselective results should be induced by the chiral pore microenvironments of catalytic sites. Ce-MDIP1 can be easily recovered and reused at least three times with almost-retained catalytic activity, enantioselectivity, and crystallinity.
## 5.3. Pore-Size-Dependent Catalytic Efficiency
The catalytic properties of MOFs are very sensitive to the pore structures, because the access of catalytic sites to reactant molecules should be transported through the open channels of MOFs. The pore size effect on the catalytic efficiency has been well documented by the ${\mathbb{W}}{\mathbb{u}}$ group.[60] The 2D structure of $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{2}(p y v c a)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot3H_{2}O}}$ $(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{pyvca})_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pyvca}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ E-5-(2-(pyridin-4-yl)vinyl)isophthalic acid) is built from $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions linked by pyvca ligands with the cavity dimensions of
$7.8\mathring\mathrm{A}\times8.9\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (Figure 29). In the crystal structure, one coordination site of the $\mathrm{{Mn^{II}}}$ ion is occupied by a labile water molecule, which is readily accessible to reactant molecules. $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pyvca}})_{2}$ catalyzes the oxidation of phenylmethanol to yield $64\%$ benzaldehyde product. The 2D MOF $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pyvca})_{2}}$ can be transformed into a 3D porous MOF $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{2}(p y s c a)(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot3H_{2}O}}$ $(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{pysca})$ ; $\mathrm{{H}}_{4}\mathrm{{pysca}}=5,5^{\prime}$ -(3,4-diphenylcyclobutane-1,2-diyl)diisophthalic acid), undergoing topochemical [2+2] cycloaddition in an SCSC manner under a UV light irradiation. The photo-cycloaddition transformation slightly increases the MOF pore volume (from $24.3\%$ for $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pyvca}})_{2}$ to $27.0\%$ for $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pysca}))}$ . It is interesting that a slight increase of pore volume can significantly improve the catalytic oxidation efficiency. The yield of oxidation product benzaldehyde is increased to $97\%$ by $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pysca})}$ catalyst under otherwise identical conditions. Moreover, the benzaldehyde yield gradually increased to $98\%$ in the recycling experiments of the 2D MOF $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{pyvac})_{2}$ under ambient light irradiation, because $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{pyvca})_{2}$ was gradually transformed into 3D $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}(\mathrm{{pysca})}$ as confirmed by the PXRD pattern and the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum. These results demonstrate that the pore structures play important roles on the catalytic performance of MOF catalysts, such as facilitating the substrate transportation to contact with catalytic sites on the pore surfaces.
## 5.4. Electronic-Effect-Dependent Catalytic Efficiency
Vermoortele et al. employed a series of benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate derivatives (bdc-X; ${\mathrm{X}}={\mathrm{H}} $ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ , F, Cl, Br, and $\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ 1 to obtain a series of isostructural MOFs UiO-66-X for the catalytic cyclization of citronellal.[61] The catalytic rate on the cyclization of citronellal is highly dependent on the electronic effect of the substituent groups in btc (Figure 30). The electron-withdrawing groups (F, Cl, Br and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{'}$ ) can enhance the catalytic rate, in which UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ represents the most active one with a 56-fold increase in rate. Control experiments with different btc derivatives demonstrate that the coordinatively unsaturated sites on $\mathrm{zr}$ are the active sites. Molecular-modeling calculations revealed that the electronic properties of substituents are able to tune the Lewis acidity of the $\mathrm{zr}$ sites and induce additional stabilizing or destabilizing effects on the reactants to adjust the adsorption and activation free energy of the reaction. In the case of UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , the stronger adsorption and the preferred electrostatic interactions between the adsorbate and the nitro groups can significantly accelerate the reaction. They have correlated the relationship between catalytic efficiency and electronic effect with the Hammett’s $\sigma_{\mathrm{m}}$ values, which resulted in a linear freeenergy relationship $(\log(k_{\mathrm{X}}/k_{\mathrm{H}})=\rho\sigma_{\mathrm{m}})$ with $\rho$ value of 2.35.
## 6. Synergistic Catalysis with Assistant Catalytic Moieties
The three isostructural porphyrinic MOFs, $[\mathrm{Mn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{MnCl}\cdot$ - OCPP) $(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]$ ·guest (ZJU-18), $[\mathrm{Mn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{Ni}{\cdot}\mathrm{OCPP})$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{8}]$ ·guest (ZJU-19) and $[\mathrm{Cd}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{MnCl}{\cdot}\mathrm{OCPP})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]$ guest (ZJU-20) are built from metal-5,10,15,20-tetrakis(3,5-biscarboxylphenyl)porphyrin $(\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{OCPP}$ , $\mathrm{\boldmath~{\cal~M}~}=\mathrm{\boldmath~{\cal~M}n^{\mathrm{III}}C l}$ Ni) bridging ligands and $\mathrm{Mn^{II}}$ or $\mathrm{Cd^{II}}$ connecting metal nodes, which consist of large pore cages of about $21.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and with the pore window of about $11.{\overset{\circ}{.}}{\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ in dimensions (Figure 31).[62] These porphyrinic MOFs display distinct catalytic properties on the selective oxidation of ethylbenzene with TBHP oxidant.
ZJU-19, consisting of $\mathrm{Mn^{II}}$ nodes as catalytic sites, oxidizes $9\%$ ethylbenzene with $80\%$ acetophenone product selectivity. ZJU-20, containing $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}.$ -porphyrins as catalytic sites, prompts the oxidation reaction with $69\%$ substrate conversion and $95\%$ product selectivity. ZJU-18, consisting of both $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ metal node and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}.$ -porphyrin catalytic sites, is highly efficient on the selective oxidation reaction $(>99\%$ yield of acetophenone) under identical conditions. The distinct catalytic properties of these porphyrinic MOFs demonstrate that $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ nodes should play important collaborative roles for improvement of the oxidation efficiency of $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -porphyrins in ZJU-18 because the catalytic results of ZJU-18 are not the simple additive results of ZJU-19 and ZJU-20 The distinct catalytic properties of these porphyrinic MOFs demonstrate that the synergistic work of $\mathrm{Mn^{II}}$ nodes and $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -porphyrins in ZJU-18 can significantly improve the catalytic efficiency. Moreover, ZJU-18 preferably takes up ethylbenzene substrate over acetophenone product (23:5 ratio) in their equivalent mixture, which can keep high substrate concentration around catalytic sites by accumulating the substrate molecules in the pore space. The selective accumulation of substrate molecules by transmission channels can further improve the catalytic efficiency of MOF catalysts. For example, the porous MOF catalyst ZJU-20 is much superior to the molecular catalyst $\mathrm{Mn^{III}C l{\cdot}M e_{8}O C P P}$ with the same $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ -porphyrin catalytic sites on the oxidation reaction, in which the ethylbenzene substrate conversions are of 69 and $16\%$ , respectively. ZJU-18 is very stable, and can be reused in successive runs for 15 cycles with almost-retained catalytic activity and structural integrity, whereas the homogeneous catalyst Mn $\mathsf{\Omega}_{1}\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{8}\mathsf{O C P P}$ basically lost its activity after three cycles due to self-oxidation.
The porphyrinic framework of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{Mn^{\mathrm{III}}O H{\cdot}T C P P}$ ) (DPNI)]·solvents (CZJ-1; DPNI = N,N′-di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8- naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide) is a doubly interpenetrated 3D porous structure, building from paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ SBUs bridged by $\mathrm{Mn^{III}O H}$ -TCPP ligands and DPNI struts with substrate accessible pore channels of $6.1\mathrm{~\AA~}\times7.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in dimensions (Figure 32).[63] CZJ-1 exhibits high catalytic activity on the epoxidation of styrene at room temperature, in which styrene substrate can be almost fully oxidized into the epoxide product $(98\%$ yield) by PhIO oxidant with TON up to 14068. Moreover, the inert cyclohexane substrate can be almost quantitatively oxidized by CZJ-1 at room temperature $94\%$ conversion). The high catalytic efficiency of CZJ-1 on these oxidation reactions should be attributed to the collaborative work of two kinds of catalytic sites, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ -porphyrins and DPNI moieties, immobilized on the pore surfaces. CZJ-1 is very stable which retains high catalytic activity in six successive runs, whereas the molecular catalyst Mn-TPP loses its activity after the second run.
Volkmer and co-workers used 1,4-bis[(3,5-dimethyl)pyrazol4-yl] as bridging ligands and redox-active $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ ions as metal connecting nodes to obtain a porous MOF MFU-1, which is a structural analogue of MOF-5.[64] To improve the catalytic efficiency, they have encapsulated N-hydroxyphthalimide (NHPI) into the inside pores of MFU-1 by solution impregnation method, which resulted in a composite catalyst NHPI $@$ MFU-1. NHPI $@$ MFU-1 is able to activate oxygen molecules for the aerobic oxidation of a range of organic substrates under mild conditions, in which the organic NHPI serves as an electrontransfer mediator, whereas the redox-active $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ ion acts as an oxidation site. This work demonstrates that the assistant catalyst NHPI plays a vital role on the aerobic oxidation of hydrocarbons, because MFU-1 is almost inert for these catalytic oxidation reactions.
The Duan group reported two homochiral MOFs Zn-BCIPs, building from three-branched TCA $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TCA}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -tricarboxyl­ triphenylamine) connecting to three-connected binuclear $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ nodes with 1D channels of $12\mathrm{~\AA~}\times16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in dimensions (Figure  33).[65] In the structures of Zn-BCIPs, the chiral L- or R-BCIP (BCIP $\begin{array}{r}{=N.}\end{array}$ -tert-butoxycarbonyl-2-(imidazole)-1-pyrrolidine) moieties are terminated on the zinc nodes. Heating deprotection of Zn-BCIPs resulted in photoactive homochiral MOFs $Z\mathrm{n}$ -PYIs, containing chiral catalytic sites of L- or R-PYI $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{(\mathrm{PYI}=}}&{{}}\end{array}$ pyrrolidine-2-ylimidazole) and chromophores of triphenylamine moieties. Zn-PYIs efficiently prompt the asymmetric photo$\alpha\mathrm{\Omega}$ -alkylation of phenylpropylaldehyde with diethyl 2-bromomalonate (up to $74\%$ yield and $92\%$ ee). Control experiments demonstrate that the integration of photoactive triphenylamine and chiral organocatalyst d- $/\mathrm{L}$ -PYI moieties into one MOF is necessary to realize highly efficient enantioselective catalysis on the light-driven $\alpha$ -alkylation reaction. $Z\mathrm{n}$ -PYIs can be easily isolated and reused at least three times with almost-retained reactivity $(70\%$ yield), enantioselectivity $(88\%$ ee), and framework integrity.
## 7. Conclusion
The significant advances on MOF catalysts are not only attributed to the easy immobilization of catalytic sites into the porous frameworks but are also ascribed to the special capacities of selective recognition, transportation, and combination with substrate molecules. Traditional synthetic methods can create MOF catalysts with metal nodes as catalytic sites; however, because the metal nodes are also the essential constituent elements of MOFs, the dissociation of labile solvent molecules and their participation in catalysis often inevitably destroy their framework structures. The most feasible synthetic strategies for MOF catalysts are incorporation of catalytic sites into organic linkers or encapsulation of catalytic moieties into the pore space of MOFs by either in situ synthesis or post-modification. The facile modifiability of MOFs allows us to systematically tune the catalytic properties with choice functional moieties. Both crystallographic and dynamic perspectives with spectroscopic characterization provide key insights into the design, rationalization, and discovery of MOF catalysts, and comprehensive understanding of the catalytic mechanisms. Even though we have to separately discuss the roles and contributions of the individual constituent elements on the catalytic properties of MOFs, the super catalytic efficiency of MOF catalysts is not the simple additive contributions of the single individuals. The high catalytic efficiency of MOFs should be attributed to the synergistic work among catalytic sites, pore microenvironments, assistant catalytic sites, transmission channels, and others.
Compared with the traditional heterogeneous catalysts, MOF catalysts have the following super characteristics: i) the catalytic functions are systematically controllable and tunable; ii) the structure–function relationship is directly visualized form the well-defined crystal structures; iii) the high dispersion and isolation of catalytic sites in the pore surface/space can maximize the utility and accessibility; and iv) the special pore microenvironments lead to substrate-selective and synergistic catalysis. These unmatched features of MOF catalysts are similar to those of the enzymes, which represent a class of ideal porous materials for biomimetic catalysis by synergizing the multiple functions from the hierarchically arranged single individuals.
As discussed in the text, the incorporation of catalytic sites into porous MOFs has reached the realm of rationality and controllability by precisely tuning the hierarchical structures and the functional constituent moieties. It is evident from the examples illustrated here that the catalytic functions of MOFs are majorly defined by the constituent moieties, which can be significantly improved by synergistic work. Since MOF catalysts have the superiority of synergizing the individual functions, the following in-depth work for MOF catalysis should be aimed at activating the inert substrate molecules by taking the advantages of controlling multiple-step synthesis with multiple-leveled functional moieties in the hierarchical porous materials. With the help of interdisciplinary research and state-of-theart characterization techniques, we are confident that these “smart” MOF catalysts with unusual catalytic properties, rival or superior to those of the natural enzymes, through “intelligent” materials design and optimization, will emerge in the near further.
